    <div class="mt-14 max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-4" data-reveal-group>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          What is the difference between a platform and a set of tools?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">A platform is a cohesive, integrated system with abstractions, APIs, and self-service capabilities. A set of tools is a collection of independent products that each team configures separately. The platform provides consistency, governance, and a unified experience that individual tools cannot.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          How long does it take to build an internal developer platform?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">A functional platform with core capabilities can be delivered in 8 to 12 weeks. A mature platform with broad adoption and deep integration typically takes 6 to 18 months, depending on organizational complexity, existing infrastructure, and team capacity. We prioritize early value and iterate.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          Do we need a dedicated platform team?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Yes. A platform team ensures the platform is maintained, documented, and improved over time. The team size depends on the scope of the platform and the number of application teams it serves. A typical ratio is one platform engineer for every 10 to 15 application developers.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-center between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          What is a golden path?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">A golden path is a curated, well-documented, and fully automated workflow for a common engineering task: creating a new service, deploying to production, configuring monitoring, or rotating credentials. It is the recommended way to do something, not the only way. Teams can deviate when needed, but the golden path provides the fastest, safest route.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          How do you measure platform success?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">We track deployment frequency, lead time, mean time to recovery, change failure rate, developer satisfaction (via survey), platform adoption rate, infrastructure cost per service, and time-to-prod for new services. These metrics map directly to business outcomes.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          How does platform engineering handle multi-cloud?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">A well-designed platform abstracts cloud-specific services behind unified APIs and IaC modules. Teams interact with the platform, not directly with cloud consoles. This makes multi-cloud deployments practical without requiring every team to be experts in each cloud provider.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          What if we already have Kubernetes?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Kubernetes is a platform substrate, not a platform itself. Most organizations running Kubernetes still lack developer self-service, golden paths, and integrated tooling. We build the layers on top of Kubernetes that make it truly usable for application teams.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          How do you ensure platform adoption?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Adoption is driven by value, not mandates. We focus on making the platform measurably better than the alternatives: faster, safer, and easier. We work with early adopter teams, gather feedback, iterate, and let organic adoption spread through demonstrated results.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          What about existing legacy systems?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Legacy systems are integrated into the platform incrementally. We use strangler fig patterns, sidecar integrations, and API facades to bring legacy workloads onto the platform without risky big-bang migrations. Some systems may never fully migrate, and the platform accommodates that.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          How does platform engineering affect team structure?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Platform engineering typically introduces or formalizes a platform team that works alongside application teams. The platform team is a multiplier, not a bottleneck. Application teams retain ownership of their services but consume standardized infrastructure and tooling from the platform.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          What is the typical ROI timeline?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Most organizations see measurable returns within 3 to 6 months: reduced deployment times, fewer environment-related incidents, and lower infrastructure costs. Full ROI, including developer productivity gains and organizational scalability, materializes within 12 to 18 months.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          How do you handle platform security?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Security is built into every layer of the platform: network policies, identity-aware access control, secrets management, encryption in transit and at rest, vulnerability scanning in CI/CD pipelines, and policy as code that prevents non-compliant configurations from reaching production.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          Can platform engineering reduce cloud costs?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Yes. Platforms reduce cloud costs through right-sizing policies, automatic cleanup of unused resources, standardized instance selection, reserved instance management, and cost allocation visibility. Our clients typically see 20 to 40 percent cloud cost reduction within the first year.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          What is the difference between Backstage and an IDP?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Backstage is a developer portal framework that provides the user interface and service catalog for an IDP. An internal developer platform is the full system: compute, networking, storage, CI/CD, secrets, observability, and the portal. Backstage is one component of a complete platform, not the platform itself.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          How do you handle platform documentation?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Documentation is treated as a first-class deliverable. We provide architecture decision records, runbooks, onboarding guides, API references, and golden path documentation. All documentation lives alongside the code and is automatically updated through CI/CD pipelines.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-lg border border-line-2 bg-paper open:border-accent transition-colors" data-reveal>
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between p-5 text-[1rem] font-medium text-ink list-none">
          What happens after the engagement ends?
          <svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-2 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div class="px-5 pb-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">Your team is equipped to operate and evolve the platform independently. We offer ongoing support and advisory retainers for organizations that want continued access to our expertise. Many clients engage us for quarterly platform reviews and major version upgrades.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

